# TNWatch — Cursor Project Rules

You are working on TNWatch, an independent civic-accountability platform for
Tamil Nadu. ALWAYS read `CONTEXT.md` in the repo root before writing any code.
It is the single source of truth.

## Always
- Read CONTEXT.md first. If your task contradicts it, stop and flag it.
- Build ONE feature at a time. Slice #1 is MLA Profiles & Performance.
- Use Pydantic v2 models for all data. No loose dicts.
- Write async backend code (FastAPI, httpx.AsyncClient, async DB driver).
- Every agent: fetch() -> parse() -> store() -> report() (writes to agent_runs).
- Every fact-bearing row MUST carry a source_url. No source, no store.
- Idempotent upserts keyed on stable IDs. Never create duplicates on re-run.
- Ship a pytest test with every agent and every API route.
- Use .env for secrets; never hardcode keys. Keep .env.example updated.
- Conventional commits: feat: / fix: / refactor: / docs: / chore:.

## Never
- Never build all four verticals at once.
- Never hardcode political facts (party seat counts, who holds power, who is CM).
  The 17th TN Assembly is volatile — always scrape current values.
- Never publish opinions or accusations. Sourced facts only.
- Never scrape disrespectfully: honour robots.txt, rate-limit, set a User-Agent.
- Never merge code you cannot explain in one sentence.
- Never store private data or paywalled/copyrighted full text.

## Conventions
- Python/DB: snake_case. TypeScript/React: camelCase.
- Folder layout: agents/ (scrapers+normaliser), backend/ (FastAPI+schema),
  frontend/ (Next.js), data/ (seeds, local SQLite), scripts/ (one-off tools),
  docs/ (architecture, data sources, build plan).
- Tests live next to the code they test or in a tests/ subfolder.

## When stuck
- Prefer asking for the data contract / design before generating large code.
- If a selector or source structure is unknown, write a small probe script first
  and inspect output before building the full agent.
